Let's observe the case when `liquidity2Claim = 0`. The caller shouldn't receive any tokens, as they have no more claimable liquidity at the moment. First, a call to `IUniswapV3PoolActions::burn()` is made with `amount = liquidity2Claim`.

The `IUniswapV3PoolActions::burn()` documentation mention the [following](https://github.com/Uniswap/v3-core/blob/main/contracts/interfaces/pool/IUniswapV3PoolActions.sol#L52):

`"Can be used to trigger a recalculation of fees owed to a position by calling with an amount of 0"`. This means that any fees owed to the position is recalculated if `liquidity2Claim = 0`.

As we can see, that amount is directly transferred to the caller from the pool.

